// t-format-resolver-14: `graphix fmt` moves an attribute from a parenthesized
// callee to the call, the guard passes it, and the program's verdict flips.
// The parser keeps no ExplicitParens around a postfix base
// (graphix-types/src/expr/parser/arithexp.rs:200-206) and the printer writes a
// decorated base bare, so the reparse hangs the decoration on the enclosing
// postfix expression. format_source compares the ornaments of both parses as
// one flat preorder list (graphix-types/src/expr/format.rs:354-389, 432-442)
// and Expr equality ignores `dec`, so a decoration that moves to the
// neighbouring node is not refused. The same layout puts `(// c\n a)[0]`'s
// comment on `a[0]`.
//
// command: graphix --no-cache design/review-2026-10-05/repro/t-format-resolver-14.gx
//          graphix fmt --stdout design/review-2026-10-05/repro/t-format-resolver-14.gx > /tmp/f.gx
//          graphix --no-cache /tmp/f.gx; graphix fmt --stdout /tmp/f.gx
// expected: fmt keeps `#[native]` on `f` (keeps the parentheses) or refuses.
// observed (HEAD c722befe, debug build):
//   the source is refused: "#[native] annotates a computation or a call, not
//   a function"; fmt exits 0 with `let y = #[native]\nf(\n    1\n);`, which
//   runs and prints 2; formatting that output again changes it
//   (`let y =\n    #[native]\n    f(1);`): not idempotent.
let f = |x: i64| x + 1;
let y = (
    #[native]
    f
)(1);
sys::exit(sys::time::after_idle(duration:100.ms, 0));
y
